\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 Fallback CLI Utilities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 If you do not want to pull in heavy native system bindings, your TUI can shell out to native system utilities on paste:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 macOS:
\f0\b0  
\f2\fs26 osascript
\f0\fs24  (AppleScript) or 
\f2\fs26 pngpaste
\f0\fs24  (
\f2\fs26 pngpaste /tmp/clip.png
\f0\fs24 )\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Linux (Wayland):
\f0\b0  
\f2\fs26 wl-paste --type image/png > /tmp/clip.png
\f0\fs24 \cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Linux (X11):
\f0\b0  
\f2\fs26 xclip -selection clipboard -t image/png -o > /tmp/clip.png
\f0\fs24 \cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Windows:
