\cf2 \cb3 Second Questikn :\
In terminal o have a TUi which I use for agent interaction is it possible to use a method where I can copy a image or file or drop a image or file similar to an app or chrome\cb1 \
\
\
\cb3 Response :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Yes, you can absolutely add an interactive "Browse" button to a terminal UI.\cb1 \
\cb3 Modern terminal emulators support mouse tracking (using SGR 1006 escape sequences), meaning your TUI framework can detect when and where a user clicks. When the user clicks the button, you have two primary architectural ways to handle the file browsing experience.\cb1 \
\cb3 Here is how you can implement both approaches depending on your framework.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 Approach 1: Spawning a Native OS File Dialog (Best for Images)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Because terminals cannot display image thumbnails easily, the most user-friendly approach for selecting images is to let the terminal button trigger the operating system's native graphical file picker (macOS Finder, Windows Explorer, or Linux GNOME/KDE dialogs).\cb1 \
\cb3 When the user clicks the button in your TUI, your app temporarily shells out to a native tool, waits for the user to select the file, and reads the returned path.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 How to implement it:
\f0\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Python:
\f0\b0  You can use 
\f2 tkinter.filedialog
\f0  in a background thread, or a library like 
\f2 plyer
\f0 .\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rust (Ratatui):
\f0\b0  Use the 
\f2 rfd
\f0  (Rusty File Dialogs) crate. It handles native OS APIs directly.\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Go (Bubble Tea):
\f0\b0  Use libraries like 
\f2 gen2brain/dlgs
\f0  or 
\f2 sqweek/dialog
\f0 .\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Universal Shell Fallback:
\f0\b0  You can execute standard OS commands directly from your TUI code:\cb1 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl1
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 macOS:
\f0\b0  Run 
\f2 osascript -e 'POSIX path of (choose file of type \{"png", "jpg"\})'
\f0 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl1
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Linux:
\f0\b0  Run 
\f2 zenity --file-selection
\f0 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl1
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Windows:
\f0\b0  Run a quick PowerShell script: 
\f2 powershell.exe -Command "Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms; $f = New-Object System.Windows.Forms.OpenFileDialog; $f.ShowDialog(); $f.FileName"
\f0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 \cb3 Flow:
\f0\i0  User clicks "Browse" -> TUI pauses -> Native window opens -> User visually picks an image -> Native window closes -> TUI resumes and captures the path string.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 Approach 2: Embedded Pure-TUI File Browser\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 If you want to keep the user strictly inside the terminal, you can render a directory tree modal directly in your TUI when the button is clicked.\cb1 \
\cb3 Here is how the major TUI frameworks handle this natively:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa265\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 Python: Textual\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Textual has robust mouse support and community packages specifically for this.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Add a standard 
\f2 <Button id="browse-btn">Browse Files</Button>
\f0  to your layout.\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Install the 
\f2 textual-fspicker
\f0  library.\cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bind the button click to push the file picker modal:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://textual.app/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 textual.app}} import App, ComposeResult\
from textual.widgets import Button, Label\
from textual_fspicker import FileOpen\
\
class AgentApp(App):\
    def compose(self) -> ComposeResult:\
        yield Button("Browse Image", id="browse-btn")\
        yield Label("No file selected", id="path-label")\
\
    def on_button_pressed(self, event: Button.Pressed) -> None:\
        if {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://event.button.id/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 event.button.id}} == "browse-btn":\
            # Push the pure-TUI file browser modal\
            self.push_screen(FileOpen("."), self.capture_path)\
\
    def capture_path(self, path: str | None) -> None:\
        if path:\
            self.query_one("#path-label").update(f"Selected: \{path\}")\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa265\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 Go: Bubble Tea\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Bubble Tea maintains an official file picker component in its 
\f2 bubbles
\f0  repository.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Import 
\f2 [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbles/filepicker%5D(https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbles/filepicker)"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 github.com/charmbracelet/bubbles/filepicker](https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbles/filepicker)}}
\f0 .\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When your model receives a 
\f2 tea.MouseMsg
\f0  indicating a click on your button's coordinates, change your application state to render the file picker.\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The file picker will draw a list of files in the terminal. When the user navigates and presses 
\f2 Enter
\f0 , the picker sends a 
\f2 filepicker.DidSelectFileMsg
\f0 .\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your 
\f2 Update()
\f0  loop catches this message, extracts the string path, hides the picker, and passes the path to your agent payload.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 Which should you choose?\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Choose 
\f1\b Approach 1 (Native OS Dialog)
\f0\b0  if your agent relies heavily on visual files. It is incredibly frustrating to select an image by its filename alone inside a terminal tree.\cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Choose 
\f1\b Approach 2 (Pure TUI)
\f0\b0  if you are selecting code files, logs, or text documents where filenames are descriptive, or if you are running the TUI over an SSH connection where a local GUI dialog isn't possible.\cb1 \
}